{
  "id": "07-open-hands",
  "title": "Open Hands",
  "tenet": "Train the heart in detachment",
  "durationMs": 73824,
  "audioAsset": "kingdom/07-open-hands.mp3",
  "transcriptAsset": "assets/kingdom/07-open-hands.json",
  "text": "Can you hear God clearly while gripping the answer you already want?\n\nA young woman prayed that God would use her voice. She pictured a crowded room listening. Then her choir leader asked her to visit an older singer who could no longer leave home. She felt the sting of being unseen. A friend asked, Would your voice still be a gift if only one person heard it? The question stayed with her. She prayed for a willing heart, visited the woman, and sang beside her chair. The song was quieter than she had imagined. Its love was no smaller.\n\nDetachment does not mean having no hopes. It means entrusting our hopes and their outcome to God, so that pride, fear, and preference do not choose for us. This takes training. Each day, name what you desire, and notice what you refuse to consider. Ask whether you would still do the good if it brought no credit. Offer the goal again in prayer. Take one small action that loosens your grip: listen before defending yourself, serve without being seen, or accept a correction. To abandon ourselves to God is not to despise the self. It is to trust his care more than our own control. Open your hands, then keep them open.",
